EXT. COUNTY FAIR - FERRIS WHEEL, TOP GONDOLA - DUSK

The wheel has stopped. Forty feet up. The gondola rocks. Far below, a corn dog stand glows.

DELPHINE OKAFOR, 40s, nurse's scrubs under a cardigan, grips the safety bar. Across from her sit her daughters, ZURI, 14, braces and a cracked phone, and BIBI, 6, tiger face paint half smeared. Beside Delphine sits her father, PAPA EMEKA, 70s, Sunday hat, calm as stone.

ZURI
Mom, we've been stuck for twenty minutes.

DELPHINE
I know, Zuri. Stay in your seat.

BIBI
Zuri's phone is shaking.

ZURI
My hand is shaking, Bibi. The phone is fine.

PAPA EMEKA
Bibi, come sit by Papa. Look at the lights, not the ground.

BIBI
I want to look at the ground.

DELPHINE
Bibi. Seat.

The gondola sways. Zuri grabs Bibi's wrist. She pulls her close and she squeezes back hard.

HANK (O.S.)
(over the loudspeaker)
Folks, generator tripped. Sit tight, we're rebooting.

ZURI
Rebooting? Papa, he said rebooting.

PAPA EMEKA
Zuri, I once rode a lorry across the Niger with no brakes. We will be fine.

BIBI
Did Papa really do that, Mom?

DELPHINE
Ask him when we are on the ground, baby.

PAPA EMEKA
Delphine, take my hat. Your hands need something to hold.

Delphine takes the hat. Turns it in her fingers.

DELPHINE
Thank you, Papa.

The wheel lurches. Everyone freezes. It holds.

ZURI
Bibi, don't look down.

BIBI
I'm looking at you, Zuri.

HANK (O.S.)
(over the loudspeaker)
Almost there, Okafors!

DELPHINE
He knows our name.

PAPA EMEKA
Small town. Everybody knows everybody.
